{
  "id": "binary-tree-zigzag-level-order-traversal",
  "questionId": "binary-tree-zigzag-level-order-traversal",
  "slug": "binary-tree-zigzag-level-order-traversal",
  "title": "Binary Tree Zigzag Level Order Traversal",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Tree bfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree bfs",
    "queue"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "this is level order traversal with a twist - can you build each level's list normally and just flip a direction flag afterward?",
    "process the queue one level at a time as usual, collecting that level's values into a list",
    "toggle a boolean each time you finish a level; when it's true, reverse the list before adding it to the result",
    "an alternative without any reversal: use a deque per level and, depending on direction, append new values to the front or the back as you pop them off the queue"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "reversing the traversal order itself (which child gets enqueued first) instead of just reversing the collected values, which can break the parent-child enqueue order for the next level",
    "toggling the direction flag before building the current level instead of after, causing an off-by-one shift in which levels get reversed",
    "forgetting that level 0 is always left to right, and starting the toggle in the wrong state"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A binary tree is given as a plain integer array named `tree`, using a complete-binary-tree (heap) index layout:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`, which means \"no node here\"\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nNode 2 has no left child but does have a right child, 4.\n\n**Group the tree's node values level by level like a normal level order traversal, but alternate the reading direction each level: level 0 is left to right, level 1 is right to left, level 2 is left to right again, and so on. Return the levels as a list of lists.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= tree.length <= 2000",
      "-100 <= tree[i] <= 100 for every real node value",
      "tree[i] == -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node tree",
      "a tree with only two levels, so only the second level is reversed",
      "a tree that is a chain, where every level has exactly one value and reversal has no visible effect"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [3, 9, 20, -101, -101, 15, 7]",
        "output": "[[3], [20, 9], [15, 7]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[3], [20, 9], [15, 7]]",
        "explanation": "level 0 (left to right) is [3]; level 1 (right to left) reverses [9, 20] into [20, 9]; level 2 (left to right) is [15, 7]"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]",
        "output": "[[1], [3, 2], [4, 5, 6, 7]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[1], [3, 2], [4, 5, 6, 7]]",
        "explanation": "level 0 is [1]; level 1 left-to-right would be [2, 3], reversed to [3, 2]; level 2 is left-to-right, [4, 5, 6, 7]"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1]",
        "output": "[[1]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[1]]",
